{"name":"Dirty Snow","hex":"#cdced5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dirty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unclean; covered with or containing unpleasant substances such as dirt or grime.","That makes one unclean; corrupting, infecting."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In a dirty manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dirty"},{"term":"snow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The partly frozen, crystalline state of water that falls from the atmosphere as precipitation in flakes; also, the falling of such flakes; and the accumulation of them on the ground or on objects as a white layer.\n\n An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm.","An instance of the falling of snow (etymology 1, noun sense 1); a snowfall; also, a snowstorm."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Preceded by the dummy subject it: to have snow (noun etymology 1, noun sense 1) fall from the atmosphere.","Of a thing: to fall like snow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Georges Simenon","blocks":[{"text":"Georges Joseph Christian Simenon was a Belgian writer who created the fictional detective Jules Maigret. One of the most prolific and successful authors of the 20th century, he published around 400 novels, 21 volumes of memoirs and many short stories, selling over 500 million copies."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2d/Georges_Simenon_%281963%29_without_hat_by_Erling_Mandelmann.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georges_Simenon"},{"title":"Dirt","blocks":[{"text":"Dirt  is any matter considered unclean, especially when in contact with a person's clothes, skin, or possessions. In such cases, they are said to become dirty.\nCommon types of dirt include:"},{"items":["Debris: scattered pieces of waste or remains","Dust: a general powder of organic or mineral matter","Filth: foul matter such as excrement","Grime: a black, ingrained dust such as soot","Soil: the mix of clay, sand, and humus which lies on top of bedrock. The term 'soil' may be used to refer to unwanted substances or dirt that are deposited onto surfaces such as clothing."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/King%27s_Cross%2C_London%3B_the_Great_Dust-Heap%2C_next_to_Battle_Br_Wellcome_V0013642.jpg/330px-King%27s_Cross%2C_London%3B_the_Great_Dust-Heap%2C_next_to_Battle_Br_Wellcome_V0013642.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirt"},{"title":"Snow","blocks":[{"text":"Snow is a form of solid atmospheric precipitation consisting of individual ice crystals that grow while suspended in the air—usually within clouds—and then fall, accumulating on the ground, where they undergo further changes. It consists of frozen crystalline water throughout its life cycle, starting when, under suitable conditions, the ice crystals form in the atmosphere, increase to millimeter size, precipitate and accumulate on surfaces, then metamorphose in place, and ultimately melt, slide, or sublimate away."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg/330px-CargoNet_Di_12_Euro_4000_L%C3%B8nsdal_-_Bolna.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snow"},{"title":"Shades of white","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of white are colors that differ slightly from the CIE standard illuminant D65, a white point that represents the average color of daylight at noon. There isn't one objectively pure white, as noon daylight varies by location and atmospheric conditions, and the choice of using D65 instead of direct sunlight or a white point on the Planckian locus is arbitrary. For simplicity, this article will use the term pure white for the D65 white point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Color_icon_white.svg/330px-Color_icon_white.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_white"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg/500px-Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg/1280px-Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg/500px-Simenon-rue_Leopold.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"26 rue Léopold, Liège, the house where Simenon was born","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASimenon-rue_Leopold.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg/500px-Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg/1280px-Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg/500px-Delfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Maigret statue in Delfzijl, Netherlands","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADelfzijl_Maigret_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Georges_Simenon_%281963%29_by_Erling_Mandelmann.jpg/250px-Georges_Simenon_%281963%29_by_Erling_Mandelmann.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Georges_Simenon_%2